//%% [instellingen] waarden=uit code=verborgen stijl=top2026/stijl.css
//%% [banner] TOP Software · 26 November 2026 | Building a Complete FP Toolchain | Jan Martin Jansen · {dia} / {n}
//%% [markdown]
// # Building a Complete FP Toolchain
//
// <p class="subtitle">An Experiment in LLM-Driven Software Development</p>
//
// <p class="byline">Jan Martin Jansen · TOP Software · 26 November 2026</p>
//%%
import "lib/list.spp" as L
//%% [markdown]
// # What are you looking at?
//
// <p class="beat">A presentation?</p>
//%% [markdown]
// # What are you looking at?
//
// <p class="beat dim">A presentation?</p>
// <p class="beat">Or a program?</p>
//%%
sieve xs = case xs (Cons p rest -> Cons p (sieve (L.filter (\x -> x % p != 0) rest)))

primes = sieve (L.from 2)
//%%
n = 12
//%%
L.take n primes
//%% [markdown]
// # Both.
//
// An **interactive document** that runs **entirely in your browser**:
// no server, no installation, and you can **edit it on the fly**.
//%%
D.slider 10 200 n
//%%
primesUpTo x = L.length (L.takeWhile (\p -> p <= x) primes)
//%%
D.lineChart [ primesUpTo x | x <- L.range 2 n ]
//%% [markdown]
// <p class="caption">Number of primes up to <i>n</i></p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## What just happened?
//
// - Every cell is a small program, **compiled when you change it** and linked
//   into the running session; only the cells that depend on it run again.
// - The compiler, the linker and the typechecker are **themselves bytecode**,
//   running on **one small VM** in WebAssembly, in this browser tab.
// - The infinite list `primes` is computed **lazily**: only as far as a cell asks.
//
// <p class="hint">Press <b>C</b> to show the code, change it, and watch the slide follow.</p>
//%%
D.table ["Step, per cell", "Runs where", "Written in"]
  [ ["Sapl+ preprocessor (records, ZF, patterns)", "this tab", "Sapl"],
    ["Typechecker", "this tab", "Sapl"],
    ["Compiler gen1 (to bytecode)", "this tab", "Sapl"],
    ["Linker, loading into the session", "this tab", "Sapl"],
    ["The VM itself", "this tab (WebAssembly)", "C"] ]
//%% [markdown]
// ## How could one person build this?
//
// About **four months** of work, since 17 July 2026:
//%%
D.table ["Since 17 July 2026", "Count"]
  [ ["Commits (plus 215 merges)", "1,213"],
    ["Lines of Sapl / Sapl+ (compiler, tools, libraries)", "~138,000"],
    ["Lines of VM (C)", "~21,000"],
    ["Design documents and reports", "153"] ]
//%% [markdown]
// - **One small VM** in C; everything else is a program running on it.
// - A **self-hosting compiler** (gen1) that compiles itself, anchored by a typed Haskell version.
// - One intermediate form, **Retag**, shared by every route: Sapl, Haskell lite and Clean.
// - **LLMs** made experiments cheap: five backends were built and *measured*, instead of argued about.
//%% [markdown]
// ## The real insight: verification, not speed
//
// LLM-written code became trustworthy through **oracles that check byte for byte**:
//
// - the **fixpoint** of gen1: the compiler must reproduce itself exactly;
// - the **typed Haskell version** as an independent anchor;
// - **two generators** (Python and Sapl) that must emit the same C;
// - the **full regression** on every change, and the documents first.
//
// <p class="hint">What went wrong, honestly: an agent reported 38 ms for the compiler compiling itself.
// It was an aborted run; the fixpoint check caught it.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## What this means for Clean
//
// 1. **Lead with the toolchain.** One small VM with everything language-related as
//    bytecode, on both sides of the wire. That answers "Clean in the browser".
// 2. **Native code is an add-on.** Via Retag to C, without touching the VM:
//    faster than Clean native on small programs, ~1.3× slower on large typed code.
// 3. **The level of the intermediate form matters more than the backend.** Bring
//    stack code (ABC, JMVM bytecode) back to a structured form, and keep the types.
//%% [markdown]
// ## Measured
//%%
D.table ["Measurement (Apple M4)", "Result", "Date"]
  [ ["Sapl via Retag to C vs. Clean native, 12 benchmarks", "0.84×", "14 Nov"],
    ["Sapl via Retag to C vs. GHC -O2, 13 benchmarks", "0.99×", "14 Nov"],
    ["Clean via ABC, Retag and C vs. Clean native, 12 benchmarks", "0.95×", "13 Nov"],
    ["Typed Clean compiler (2,700 functions) compiling itself", "223 vs. 163 ms (1.37×)", "13-14 Nov"],
    ["  the same, without tag checks", "211 ms (1.30×)", "14 Nov"],
    ["GHC on the same task, default vs. tuned nursery", "271 vs. 226 ms", "13 Nov"],
    ["Clean's ABC interpreter, C via Emscripten vs. generated wasm", "0.71× (preliminary, V8)", "14 Nov"] ]
//%% [markdown]
// <p class="hint">Lower is faster. Clean native ran in a Linux VM on the same machine.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## What has not been measured
//
// - A real **iTasks** application: speed, and **compile time** (the typed self-compile
//   is 3.7 MB of C and takes 4.2 s in clang).
// - **Uniqueness and arrays**, records, `ccall`, dynamics.
// - The native route in **WebAssembly**, and graph compatibility with the ABC interpreter.
// - **Firefox and Safari** for the ABC interpreter variants; Windows.
//%% [markdown]
// ## A proposal
//
// One day together: take **one real iTasks program** through the abc2retag route,
// and measure speed, compile time and graph compatibility.
//
// <p class="beat">Questions?</p>
